  "title": "Static Analysis for Efficient Streaming Tokenization",
  "abstract": "Tokenization, also referred to as lexing or scanning, is the computational task of partitioning an input text into a sequence of substrings called tokens. Tokenization is one of the first stages of program compilation, it is used in natural language processing, and it is also useful for processing unstructured text or semi-structured data such as JSON, CSV, and XML. A tokenizer is typically specified as a list of regular expressions, which is called a tokenization grammar. Each regular expression describes a class of tokens (e.g., integer, floating-point number, variable identifier, string literal). The semantics of tokenization employs the longest match policy to disambiguate among the possible choices. This policy says that we should prefer a longer token over a shorter one. It is also known as the maximal munch policy.",
